  clock: 20  # 7e23
  nonce: '7e23'  # 7e23
  message: "Zig tail-language slice landed (PR merged, dev 53e7aaa7f3). zig.py now emits kind=field for container_field (the shared node for struct fields, tagged-union fields, and enum variants; owner threaded through the traversal stack, so a nested Inner.x and an Outer.y are attributed correctly) and kind=variable for a module-level non-type, non-@import variable_declaration (gated on parent.type == source_file, so function-body locals under a block and struct-associated consts under an aggregate body are structurally excluded — no scope walk). ZigAnalyzer.register_symbol skips field/variable (the canonical WI-jusus chokepoint) so a data anchor never enters the resolution registry; the base orchestrator still collects them into output from analysis.symbols. TDD Red-to-Green, 100 percent extended-package coverage in isolation; chokepoint verified behaviorally (a module var value coexisting with fn value() leaves the value() call resolving to the function). Additive symbols only (taint- and coherence-neutral). WI-jusus STAYS todo_hard (the non-gated analyzer tail continues). Tail progression so far: kotlin, scala, dart, solidity, julia, zig. Remaining tail: nim, d_lang, fortran, elixir, fsharp, plus the julia non-const module variables deferred earlier. IMPORTANT FINDING for the next slice: haskell was assessed and is NOT a clean fit for this recipe — Haskell record fields compile to accessor FUNCTIONS and top-level bindings are application targets, so the 'field/variable are never call targets' premise (the whole basis of the register_symbol chokepoint) does not hold; unregistering them would break call/reference resolution. A Haskell slice needs a different design and is deferred."  # 7e23
- op: discuss  # 1606
  at: '2026-07-06T06:42:04Z'  # 1606
  by: agent  # 1606
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1606
  clock: 21  # 1606
  nonce: '1606'  # 1606
  message: "Nim tail slice landed (PR #451, dev 125603aba6). nim.py now emits kind='field' for object 'field_declaration' + 'enum_field_declaration' members (qualified Owner.member; '*' export marker via the reused INV-bisom '_declared_name_node') and kind='variable' for module-level const/var/let 'variable_declaration' (gated on its section sitting directly under 'source_file', so proc-body locals — whose section sits under 'statement_list' — are structurally excluded; no scope walk needed since object/enum fields use DISTINCT grammar nodes). Call-graph integrity closed at the 'NimAnalyzer.register_symbol' chokepoint (skips field/variable) + never added to symbol_by_name.\n\nADVERSARIAL REVIEW (4 refuters, NO-GO -> fixed -> GO) caught a confidently-wrong emission hidden by 100% line coverage (the INV-vomuf/closure-evidence failure mode): an inline anonymous tuple used as a field TYPE ('coord: tuple[x, y: int]') is modelled with the tuple's OWN nested 'field_declaration' nodes, so the tuple's members leaked onto the enclosing object as phantom wrong-owner fields (Outer.x/Outer.y), and on a member-name collision emitted a duplicate Owner.member. Fixed structurally in '_enclosing_type_name': abort the owner walk (fails-safe skip) if it crosses an intervening 'field_declaration' before the 'type_declaration' — verified against the bundled grammar to drop exactly the phantoms while PRESERVING real object fields, object-variant 'case'-branch fields (Shape.radius/Shape.side), and standalone named-tuple members ('type Coord = tuple[x,y:int]' -> Coord.x/Coord.y; the rejected 'intervening tuple_type' alternative would have wrongly dropped these). Documented fails-safe recall misses (skip, never mis-emit): the object-variant DISCRIMINATOR field ('case kind: Kind', a variant_discriminator_declaration) and a 'when defined(...): var x' module variable.\n\n61 tests, 100% coverage (in-suite + extended package isolation); smart-test 4380 passed. Live repro confirmed: 'Outer = object; coord: tuple[x,y:int]' now yields field Outer.coord only. Remaining WI-jusus tail: d_lang, fortran, elixir, fsharp, + julia NON-const module variables (needs scope discrimination). Stays todo_hard; awaits_bakeoff_validation retained."  # 1606
- op: discuss  # ee9d
  at: '2026-07-06T07:39:55Z'  # ee9d
  by: agent  # ee9d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ee9d
  clock: 22  # ee9d
  nonce: ee9d  # ee9d
  message: "D (d_lang) tail slice landed (PR #453, dev 349f4de701). d_lang.py now emits kind='field' for struct/class/interface + enum members (qualified Owner.member) and kind='variable' for module-level globals. Scope discrimination keys on the IMMEDIATE parent since a field, a module global, and a function-body local are all the same 'variable_declaration' node ('auto x' is a distinct 'auto_declaration'): parent 'aggregate_body' -> field (owner via the existing '_find_parent_container', nearest-type walk); module scope ('module_def' with a 'module X;' decl, else 'source_file' directly) -> variable; anything else (block_statement/function_body) -> skipped local. enum_member -> field owned by the enclosing 'enum_declaration'; an anonymous enum parses to a distinct 'anonymous_enum_declaration' (no owner) and is skipped. Multi-name declarators handled. Call-graph integrity via the 'DAnalyzer.register_symbol' chokepoint (skips field/variable before its module-qualified + bare-name registrations).\n\nADVERSARIAL REVIEW (4 refuters, NO-GO -> fixed -> GO) again caught a confidently-wrong emission hidden by 100% line coverage (the D analogue of the Nim inline-tuple phantom): a NAMED union nested in a struct/class ('struct Outer { union U { int a; } }') declares a nested TYPE, so its members belong to U ('U.a'), but '_find_parent_container' walked PAST the union (absent from '_CONTAINER_NODE_TYPES') and mis-attributed them to Outer (phantom 'Outer.a'). Fixed by adding 'union_declaration' to '_CONTAINER_NODE_TYPES': a named union now owns its members ('U.a'/'U.b'); an ANONYMOUS union has no 'identifier' child, so the owner walk's 'if name_node' guard skips it and correctly keeps hoisting members into the enclosing struct ('Anon.x'); a top-level named union's members are now recalled ('TopU.p'); the same fix also correctly qualifies methods declared inside a named union. Live repro after fix: 'struct Outer { union U { int a; float b; } int c; }' -> U.a/U.b/Outer.c (no Outer.a/Outer.b).\n\n28 tests, 100% coverage (in-suite + extended package isolation); smart-test all files 100%. Remaining WI-jusus tail: fortran, elixir, fsharp (F# has a bundled 'value'-kind local-leak to fix + a value->variable reclassification, a separate focused slice), + julia NON-const module variables. Stays todo_hard; awaits_bakeoff_validation retained."  # ee9d
- op: discuss  # 59c5
  at: '2026-07-06T08:40:00Z'  # 59c5
  by: agent  # 59c5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 59c5
  clock: 23  # 59c5
  nonce: 59c5  # 59c5
  message: "F# (fsharp) tail slice landed (PR #454, dev 1b78182935). Emits kind='field' for record members (owner = record type; 'mutable' modifier skipped) and RECLASSIFIES module-level 'let' value bindings from the F#-only kind='value' to the cross-language kind='variable' ('value' had no cross-package consumer, no test asserted it). A scope guard '_fsharp_value_is_module_level' fixes a PRE-EXISTING local-leak bug (the old value emission had no scope check and leaked function-body locals): only a value whose function_or_value_defn sits under a module scope (named_module/module_defn/file) emits; a function-body local (grandparent function_or_value_defn) is excluded even in a headerless file.\n\nDESIGN NUANCE (F#-specific, surfaced + corrected by the 4-refuter adversarial review, verdict GO_WITH_CHANGE): the register_symbol chokepoint here skips ONLY 'field', NOT 'variable' — unlike the imperative-language slices (nim/d/zig/etc. skip both). Two reasons the review VERIFIED against HEAD: (1) a record 'field' MUST stay out of resolution because the NameResolver suffix index would suffix-match a qualified 'Rec.name' to a bare call 'name(...)' and mint a wrong calls edge (load-bearing precision guard); (2) an F# 'variable' is a legitimately CALLABLE let-binding (point-free 'let inc = (+) 1', lambda 'let add = fun a b -> ...', composition, factory results) and F# forbids a same-name value+function in a module (no clobber), so excluding variables from resolution was a REGRESSION (dropped calls edges that resolved at 0.85 before) — variables stay resolvable.\n\nThe review caught TWO regressions vs the first cut, both fixed + locked with regression tests: (a) a phantom 'anonymous_module' scope-allowlist entry (never produced by the bundled grammar) that omitted the real 'file' root, silently dropping every top-level 'let' value in headerless .fsx scripts / implicit-module .fs files — fixed to use 'file'; (b) the variable-exclusion recall regression above — fixed to skip-only-field. Live repro after fixes: headerless 'script.fsx' with 'let apiKey' -> variable apiKey (localBinding local excluded); 'let add = fun a b -> a+b' + 'let caller x = add x 1' -> caller->add calls edge resolves; zero value-kind symbols.\n\n40 tests, 100% coverage (in-suite + common package isolation); smart-test all changed sources 100%. NOTE: kind='value' is now a registered-but-unemitted SymbolKindSpec (its sole producer was F#); left registered (conservative — like many unemitted kinds), not removed. Remaining WI-jusus tail: fortran (additive but no _make_symbol helper + a 3-way-shared variable_declaration scope classifier + an under-tested scope-walk), elixir (structural mismatch: defstruct macro / @attributes), + julia NON-const module variables. Stays todo_hard; awaits_bakeoff_validation retained."  # 59c5
- op: discuss  # 2fd5
  at: '2026-07-06T12:40:10Z'  # 2fd5
  by: agent  # 2fd5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2fd5
  clock: 24  # 2fd5
  nonce: 2fd5  # 2fd5
  message: "Fortran tail-language slice DONE — merged dev 1d421f84f6 (PR feat(fortran), commit b9e3828099). Emits kind=field for derived-type components (owner=type, e.g. point.x) and kind=variable for module/program/submodule/block_data-level declarations, classified by EFFECTIVE scope (_effective_scope walks past transparent preproc_* conditionals so #ifdef-guarded decls in .F90 classify correctly). Names harvested across all 5 tree-sitter declarator shapes (identifier / sized_declarator arrays grid(nx,ny) / coarray_declarator field[*] / pointer_init_declarator q(:)=>tgt binding the NAME not the target / init_declarator) via leftmost-child recursion; PDT kind/len params excluded. Call-graph integrity at one chokepoint (FortranAnalyzer.register_symbol skips BOTH field+variable — Fortran is imperative: a module var 'compute' would exact-match a bare 'call compute()' clobbering a same-named subroutine, and a field would suffix-match). Additive symbols only.\n\nNOTABLE: this slice took SEVEN adversarial-review rounds, each catching a real, blocking, coverage-hidden defect — the strongest demonstration in the campaign of 'line coverage is not correctness; the diverse-lens review is the gate'. R1-4: recall loss on the dominant Fortran idiom (inline arrays/pointer-init/init-arrays silently dropped), phantom PDT kind/len fields, coarrays, preprocessor-guarded decls, wrong-owner nested-type phantom. R5 exposed the LOAD-BEARING durable lesson: the bundled tree_sitter_fortran grammar sets has_error on VALID F2003 it can't parse (asynchronous, ubiquitous in MPI-3), so ANY has_error-based deferral silently drops valid declarations — forcing a redesign onto an explicit FIRM CONTRACT (never drop a valid symbol on compilable code; never emit a wrong edge; suppress phantoms only via signals that cannot fire on compilable code). R6-7 removed the last over-eager guards (prev-sibling + next-sibling ERROR guards) that each dropped a valid decl (standalone asynchronous/codimension statement follower; asynchronous-as-last-statement whole-unit drop, fixed via ERROR-wrapped-unit scope recovery; fixed-form .f column-6 continuation clean leading name), converging on minimal provably-relaxation-biased logic (harvest names, stop at first in-vardecl ERROR child — a grammar-parseable decl has no ERROR child, so no valid symbol on compilable code is ever dropped). Accepted residuals (spurious EXTRA, never a wrong edge; validated to have NO safe suppression signal): DEC/VAX STRUCTURE/UNION/MAP phantoms (non-standard legacy the grammar can't parse); a name buried inside an ERROR node (fixed-form CONTINUED name, inline-asynchronous name) is an irreducible grammar recall limit. 96 tests, 100% coverage (in-suite + package-isolation), + 11 proactive grammar-divergence/fixed-form firm-contract cases.\n\nDURABLE LESSON for the remaining tail: has_error is NOT a safe malformed-signal when the bundled grammar diverges from the language — deferral must use only signals that cannot fire on compilable code. WI-jusus stays todo_hard + awaits_bakeoff_validation. Remaining tail: elixir (functional-ish — re-derive the register_symbol skip-set, do NOT inherit the imperative {field,variable}), julia NON-const module variables (scope discrimination). Haskell remains UNFIT (record fields are accessor functions; top-level bindings are application targets)."  # 2fd5
- op: discuss  # 813e
  at: '2026-07-06T14:55:51Z'  # 813e
  by: agent  # 813e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 813e
  clock: 25  # 813e
  nonce: 813e  # 813e
  message: "Julia module-variable tail slice DONE — merged dev (PR feat(julia): emit kind=variable for non-const module bindings). Extends the earlier julia struct-field slice. Emits kind=variable for a non-const module-level assignment, classified by EFFECTIVE scope via a walk (_julia_variable_scope): ascends through scope-TRANSPARENT wrappers (module-level begin…end, if…elseif…else, a global x= statement, a chained x=y=5 link) to module_definition/source_file, STOPS at the first scope-introducing ancestor (function_definition incl. short-form f(x)=begin…end, let/for/while/try/do/local). const's parent is const_statement (not double-emitted). LHS name extraction: identifier; the PRE-:: target of typed_expression ((a,b)::Point -> a,b never the type); every element of open_tuple/tuple/parenthesized incl. splat_expression (rest... -> rest); a call_expression LHS (possibly typed-wrapped f(x)::Int=) is a short-form FUNCTION; field/index_expression is a MUTATION. Deduped per (scope,name).\n\nDURABLE LESSON (register-worthy refinement of the per-language skip-set rule): register_symbol skips BOTH field AND variable for Julia. This REFINES the earlier '{field}-only for functional / {field,variable} for imperative' framing. The real discriminant is REGRESSION-vs-NEW-FEATURE, not functional-vs-imperative: F# kept variables resolvable ({field}-only) because F# value bindings were ALREADY resolvable, so excluding them would DROP existing calls edges (a regression). Julia variables were UNEMITTED before this slice, so excluding them from resolution adds no edge and drops none (NO regression) — AND resolving them is actively hazardous: a variable's name is BARE/unqualified, so it EXACT-matches a bare foo() call and beats a same-named function's module-QUALIFIED suffix-match, minting a wrong cross-module calls edge (the clobber the review reproduced). So for a functional-ish language, ask 'does excluding the variable drop an EXISTING edge?' — if yes ({field}-only, like F#), if no ({field,variable}, like Julia). Elixir next: re-derive from THIS question (its @attributes/defstruct-field bindings are also new — likely {field,variable}).\n\nADVERSARIALLY REVIEWED 5 rounds (NO_GO x2 -> GO_WITH_CHANGE x2 -> GO), each caught a real coverage-hidden defect: (1) typed_expression LHS emitting the TYPE as a phantom variable clobbering the struct ((a,b)::Point -> variable:Point; f(x)::Int=x dropping the function); (2) scope gate over-excluding valid module vars behind begin/if/global/chain wrappers; (3) a short-form-function body-local leak I INTRODUCED by making assignment transparent (fixed: stop the walk at a call_expression-LHS assignment); (4) a dropped splat binding; (5) the cross-module clobber that drove the corrected {field,variable} skip. Documented fails-safe misses: in-function global, macro-wrapped @show, g=(q=5). 102 tests, 100% coverage (in-suite + package-isolation).\n\nWI-jusus stays todo_hard + awaits_bakeoff_validation. Remaining tail: elixir (structural mismatch — defstruct macro fields + @attributes). Haskell UNFIT."  # 813e
- op: discuss  # f20d
  at: '2026-07-06T16:25:28Z'  # f20d
  by: agent  # f20d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f20d
  clock: 26  # f20d
  nonce: f20d  # f20d
  message: "Elixir defstruct-field tail slice DONE — merged dev (PR #457, feat(elixir): emit kind=field for defstruct fields). SCOPED to defstruct fields (the clean, unambiguous part); @attributes->variable is a DELIBERATE documented scope-out (separating data attrs from the open-ended reserved/framework meta set — @moduledoc/@doc/@spec/@behaviour/@derive/Ecto @primary_key/... — is a real design decision).\n\nElixir has NO field/variable DECLARATION nodes — a struct's shape is the defstruct MACRO CALL. Emits kind=field per defstruct field, owner = enclosing module (an Elixir struct IS its module, %User{}). _extract_defstruct_field_names handles both syntactic forms (parenthesized or not, via args.named_children[0] skipping the ( ) tokens): list atom/quoted_atom, keywords/in-list pair KEY (never the default value), and tuple-literal pair [{:name,0}] (what [name: 0] desugars to). Value-atoms + nested keyword/map defaults never leak. Two fails-safe owner guards: a defstruct inside a quote (an __using__ template injected into a DIFFERENT module via use) emits nothing (_has_quote_ancestor); one whose nearest enclosing module is atom-named (defmodule :\"x\", unresolvable by the owner walk) or absent is dropped rather than mis-attributed to a resolvable ancestor. register_symbol skips field (qualified User.name would suffix-match a bare name() call).\n\nADVERSARIALLY REVIEWED 3 rounds (GO_WITH_CHANGE -> GO_WITH_CHANGE -> GO): R1 caught a BLOCKING 100%-recall-loss on the parenthesized defstruct([...])/defstruct(k: v) form (arguments node's first child is the ( token, not the container — fixed with named_children) + tuple-literal/quoted-key recall gaps; R2 caught the quote/__using__ phantom-owner. All caught behind 100% line coverage (only paren-less forms were tested). 129 tests, 100% coverage (in-suite + package-isolation).\n\nFiled INV-sinah (analyzer-wide quote-attribution gap: def/defmacro inside a quote are mis-attributed to the defining module identically — cross-cutting, not owned by this slice). CI note: the slice's new Python helper functions changed hypergumbo's self-analysis symbol counts (36017->36413), tripping verify-generated (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md); recovered by regenerating ARCHITECTURE.md + AGit-update-push to PR #457 + merge-pr (the amend-orphan-gotcha recovery).\n\nWI-jusus stays todo_hard + awaits_bakeoff_validation. THE TAIL'S TRACTABLE LANGUAGES ARE NOW COVERED: fortran (7 rounds, firm-contract redesign), julia non-const module vars (5 rounds, regression-vs-new-feature skip-set refinement), elixir defstruct fields (3 rounds). Haskell remains UNFIT (record fields are accessor functions; top-level bindings are application targets). Remaining WI-jusus follow-ups: elixir @attributes (deferred, needs the reserved-attr design decision), julia const-emitter gaps (INV-vopap), the analyzer-wide quote attribution (INV-sinah)."  # f20d
